<PageLayout
  title="How we test"
  description="What counts as a tested tool on MorningStacks, how we score, what we re-test, and what we will not accept money for."
  eyebrow="Methodology"
  ogSlug="methodology"
>
  <p>
    This page exists so you can hold the publication accountable. If a review does not live up to what is described below, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and we will fix it or pull it.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts as tested</h2>
  <p>Every product we review meets one of two thresholds:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Production use.</strong> The software has been used in real work for at least one full pricing cycle, typically 30 days. For team tools, that is 30 days with at least three people.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Structured trial.</strong> When production use is impractical, we run a 30-day structured trial against a published checklist for that category. The trial uses a representative use case, not an idealized one.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The last-tested date on a review is the last day the software was used in earnest. If it is older than nine months, we re-test before we promote the piece again.
  </p>

  <h2>How we score</h2>
  <p>
    Ratings are out of 5.0, in increments of 0.1. They are a judgment, not an average of sub-scores. A 4.0 means we would recommend the tool in the right context. A 5.0 means we would recommend it in any context. There is currently nothing on the site at 5.0.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not publish scores out of 100. Software is judged, not benchmarked. A high score is a recommendation, not a measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>What we will not accept money for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Reviewing a product. We review what is worth reviewing.</li>
    <li>A higher score on an existing review.</li>
    <li>Including a product in a roundup.</li>
    <li>Removing a competitor from a roundup.</li>
    <li>Letting a vendor review a piece before it publishes. We may share a fact-check excerpt. We do not share the whole piece.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We accept affiliate commissions, paid by the vendor when a reader buys after clicking through. The commission rate has no influence on scoring.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    When we get something wrong (a feature, a price, a product that changed), we update the piece and add a dated correction note. We do not silently rewrite published reviews.
  </p>

  <h2>Conflicts</h2>
  <p>
    If the writer has a personal investment in, advisory role with, or significant financial relationship to a vendor, that writer does not write the review. If you spot a conflict we missed, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
